1
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,050
Let's go ahead and get started.

2
00:00:10,050 --> 00:00:13,920
Here, we're going to talk
about load balancing.

3
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:15,940
We're going to give a
little introduction to it.

4
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:19,320
We're going to break this
material into two parts,

5
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:22,640
so it doesn't become too long.

6
00:00:23,570 --> 00:00:26,220
I guess the first question is,

7
00:00:26,220 --> 00:00:28,710
what is load balancing?

8
00:00:28,710 --> 00:00:31,020
In case you don't know,

9
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:33,380
a load balancer serves

10
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:36,640
as the point of
entry for a service,

11
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:39,500
and it directs traffic to one of

12
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:43,105
N servers that can
handle the request.

13
00:00:43,105 --> 00:00:48,585
It's used for both scaling
and for resilience.

14
00:00:48,585 --> 00:00:52,935
Scaling meaning, I have
three servers now,

15
00:00:52,935 --> 00:00:55,035
I want to add a fourth because I

16
00:00:55,035 --> 00:00:57,450
have a lot of traffic
to my servers,

17
00:00:57,450 --> 00:00:58,670
great, I can add

18
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:01,150
a fourth and the load
balancer will handle that.

19
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:04,005
It also handles resilience,

20
00:01:04,005 --> 00:01:07,695
in this case let's say
I have three servers,

21
00:01:07,695 --> 00:01:09,815
one of them fails, well,

22
00:01:09,815 --> 00:01:12,950
the load balancer can
detect that and stop

23
00:01:12,950 --> 00:01:17,810
directing traffic to
the third server.

24
00:01:18,070 --> 00:01:24,800
So a key property of load
balancing is the algorithm,

25
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:29,015
how it's doing the
balancing of the load.

26
00:01:29,015 --> 00:01:36,250
The clients of this service,
initiate a connection.

27
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:38,680
It comes to the load balancer,

28
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:41,870
and then has to

29
00:01:41,870 --> 00:01:45,880
choose which server to
forward the request to,

30
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:49,230
and so this would
be some algorithm.

31
00:01:49,230 --> 00:01:51,330
It could be round-robin,

32
00:01:51,330 --> 00:01:54,230
first connection
goes to the top one,

33
00:01:54,230 --> 00:01:56,700
second connection
goes to the middle,

34
00:01:56,700 --> 00:01:58,920
third one goes to
the bottom one,

35
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:00,650
and then it goes back,

36
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:03,030
the next connection will
go to this top one.

37
00:02:03,030 --> 00:02:06,380
It could be look at the
amount of load they have,

38
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:08,780
the number of connections
each of these has,

39
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:11,935
and divide up the traffic
in different ways.

40
00:02:11,935 --> 00:02:14,880
That's the first key
property is the algorithm.

41
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:18,330
The second is the server set.

42
00:02:18,330 --> 00:02:23,160
This is basically on
the load balancer.

43
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:26,660
You need a table of
what all servers are

44
00:02:26,660 --> 00:02:31,055
part of this set that it
can load balance between.

45
00:02:31,055 --> 00:02:37,630
This gets updated as you
add and remove servers,

46
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:40,300
but it also keeps
track of which of

47
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:44,170
these servers are live or not.

48
00:02:44,170 --> 00:02:49,150
That, would include some
form of liveness check,

49
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:52,240
so potentially
through a heartbeat.

50
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:57,130
The load balancer sends
the server requests,

51
00:02:57,130 --> 00:03:00,150
like just are you still alive?

52
00:03:00,150 --> 00:03:01,970
And if the server responds,

53
00:03:01,970 --> 00:03:05,125
it marks that down and say,
yes, it's still alive.

54
00:03:05,125 --> 00:03:10,650
This could be in the
case of a dead server,

55
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:12,790
it just wouldn't
receive the response,

56
00:03:12,790 --> 00:03:15,580
and it would time out.

57
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:18,600
That's the second key property.

58
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:21,240
Now, if we were to go into

59
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:23,960
Google Cloud platform into

60
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:27,000
the console and start saying,
give me a load balancer.

61
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,270
It's going to ask you a bunch
of different things like,

62
00:03:29,270 --> 00:03:31,010
what type are you looking for?

63
00:03:31,010 --> 00:03:34,660
The first thing is
differentiating between,

64
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:37,340
is this an application
load balancer

65
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:40,595
or is it network load balancer?

66
00:03:40,595 --> 00:03:42,280
Let's tap into
this a little bit,

67
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:45,010
make sure that we have an
understanding of both.

68
00:03:45,010 --> 00:03:48,085
When we say network
load balancer,

69
00:03:48,085 --> 00:03:50,815
and it said L4 for Layer 4,

70
00:03:50,815 --> 00:03:53,255
this is the transport layer.

71
00:03:53,255 --> 00:03:58,845
As a refresher of what
transport layer does is,

72
00:03:58,845 --> 00:04:01,710
I have an application

73
00:04:01,710 --> 00:04:05,060
run-in in this
picture on my laptop,

74
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:09,035
and there's some application
running on a server.

75
00:04:09,035 --> 00:04:11,360
Both of those are
just a process,

76
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:13,160
and so a transport layer

77
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:16,445
provides this process to
process communication,

78
00:04:16,445 --> 00:04:18,480
and in the case of TCP,

79
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:22,250
it'll provide a reliable,
in order stream.

80
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:24,960
If I'm talking to YouTube,

81
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:30,155
I open a TCP connection
between my process,

82
00:04:30,155 --> 00:04:34,180
and a process running in
the YouTube infrastructure,

83
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:36,435
and the transport layer,

84
00:04:36,435 --> 00:04:40,390
make sure that
communication can happen.

85
00:04:41,630 --> 00:04:45,830
One key part, but well
we're particularly

86
00:04:45,830 --> 00:04:47,630
interested in is one

87
00:04:47,630 --> 00:04:50,220
of the header fields
called the port.

88
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:55,755
This is an example,
transport header,

89
00:04:55,755 --> 00:04:58,210
and it's got a source port,

90
00:04:58,210 --> 00:05:01,990
a destination port, and
then some other fields.

91
00:05:01,990 --> 00:05:04,755
This would be within
an IP packet.

92
00:05:04,755 --> 00:05:08,600
What this allows the
operating system

93
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:12,660
to be able to do is to be
able to direct traffic,

94
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:16,790
either to application
that's running HTTP,

95
00:05:16,790 --> 00:05:18,395
and that would be Port 80,

96
00:05:18,395 --> 00:05:22,505
or an application running SSH,

97
00:05:22,505 --> 00:05:24,460
and that would be
Port 22 because

98
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:28,040
both of them have
the same IP address.

99
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:32,360
We've seen this with
the firewall rules,

100
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:35,300
but just to refresh it again.

101
00:05:36,010 --> 00:05:38,210
On other hand,

102
00:05:38,210 --> 00:05:44,380
an application layer protocol
defines message syntax,

103
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:46,790
what fields it has,

104
00:05:46,790 --> 00:05:48,830
how fields are delineated,

105
00:05:48,830 --> 00:05:52,010
the types of messages
that are exchange,

106
00:05:52,010 --> 00:05:54,900
is this a request and
response protocol?

107
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:57,210
And the message semantics,

108
00:05:57,210 --> 00:05:59,960
the meaning of the
information in the fields.

109
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:06,050
An example of an application
layer protocol is HTTP,

110
00:06:06,050 --> 00:06:09,935
which is the web's
application layer protocol,

111
00:06:09,935 --> 00:06:13,970
where a client and server
exchange web objects,

112
00:06:13,970 --> 00:06:18,675
whether it's HTML or JPEG,
audio file, whatever.

113
00:06:18,675 --> 00:06:20,670
The server itself is stateless,

114
00:06:20,670 --> 00:06:21,760
in that it doesn't retain

115
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:26,230
information about
past client requests.

116
00:06:26,450 --> 00:06:28,920
When you're doing a request,

117
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:32,540
you specify the
protocol like HTTP,

118
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:37,330
a host name, and then
that web object.

119
00:06:37,330 --> 00:06:42,360
In this case, the PC would
send a request to the server,

120
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:44,320
get a response, something else

121
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:47,390
can send a request
and get a response.

122
00:06:47,820 --> 00:06:51,260
Coming back to
application load balancer

123
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:53,760
versus network load balancer,

124
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:57,700
Layer 4 considers
just the network IP

125
00:06:57,700 --> 00:07:00,880
and the transport layer headers.

126
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:04,115
It can pass through the traffic,

127
00:07:04,115 --> 00:07:08,120
and the routing consideration
is largely how to

128
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:13,275
balance load and may be used
to ensure flow affinity.

129
00:07:13,275 --> 00:07:15,735
We'll come back to what that

130
00:07:15,735 --> 00:07:17,900
really means and how

131
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:20,140
Google handles it with
their Maglev system,

132
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:23,680
but it just means that if I
open up a TCP connection,

133
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:25,760
I want all packets in

134
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:29,395
that same connection to
go to the same server.

135
00:07:29,395 --> 00:07:34,120
We need to ensure the
affinity of a given flow,

136
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:36,340
meaning a TCP connection,

137
00:07:36,340 --> 00:07:38,460
all goes to the same server.

138
00:07:38,460 --> 00:07:45,050
Layer 7 also considers
the application header.

139
00:07:45,050 --> 00:07:48,475
A lot of times this is
called a web proxy.

140
00:07:48,475 --> 00:07:53,360
A lot of times it will
terminate the TCP connection,

141
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:57,380
and then it also provides
extra routing consideration,

142
00:07:57,380 --> 00:08:01,920
where you can direct specific
URLs to specific servers,

143
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:05,955
so maybe sign up
goes to Server 1,

144
00:08:05,955 --> 00:08:08,250
but then the purchase process,

145
00:08:08,250 --> 00:08:16,515
the URL of domain/purchase
goes to Servers 2 or 3.

146
00:08:16,515 --> 00:08:22,750
A web proxy or application
load balancer can do that.

147
00:08:22,910 --> 00:08:27,390
Another thing that
Google asked you about,

148
00:08:27,390 --> 00:08:31,740
is this internal or external?

149
00:08:31,740 --> 00:08:36,645
External load balancing would

150
00:08:36,645 --> 00:08:41,115
likely want a public IP
address associated with it,

151
00:08:41,115 --> 00:08:44,320
and to be able to direct it to,

152
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:49,090
in this case say,
web tier front-end.

153
00:08:49,090 --> 00:08:55,385
Internal load balancing would
be between applications,

154
00:08:55,385 --> 00:09:00,150
both of them are within
the Google domain.

155
00:09:00,260 --> 00:09:03,160
The key difference is comes back

156
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:06,830
to that Andromeda
software that we saw.

157
00:09:06,830 --> 00:09:09,445
With an internal
network load balancer,

158
00:09:09,445 --> 00:09:14,095
forwarding rules can be
handled with Andromeda.

159
00:09:14,095 --> 00:09:18,020
That's shown in this picture.

160
00:09:18,020 --> 00:09:21,185
We saw this the
same with Cloudnet.

161
00:09:21,185 --> 00:09:23,960
The load balancer
normally would have

162
00:09:23,960 --> 00:09:27,800
this separate machine in

163
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:32,580
the case of Google because
they control both sides,

164
00:09:32,580 --> 00:09:35,060
they don't need
this middle box or

165
00:09:35,060 --> 00:09:40,160
this middle proxy to do that
for a network load balancer.

166
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:45,400
The two key systems in GCP,

167
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:47,080
we're going to
learn about Maglev

168
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:48,920
for network load balancing,

169
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:51,330
for traffic from the external.

170
00:09:51,330 --> 00:09:54,270
For application load balancing,

171
00:09:54,270 --> 00:09:57,320
it's going to be set up a proxy,

172
00:09:57,320 --> 00:10:01,060
and there's some open source
software called Envoy.

173
00:10:01,970 --> 00:10:05,580
To summarize what we
just covered was,

174
00:10:05,580 --> 00:10:07,905
application or Layer 7,

175
00:10:07,905 --> 00:10:11,490
versus network or Layer
4 load balancing,

176
00:10:11,490 --> 00:10:15,870
and what it means to be
internal versus external,

177
00:10:15,870 --> 00:10:19,800
and how that differs for Google.

178
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:22,365
We're going to stop here,

179
00:10:22,365 --> 00:10:27,580
and then jump into
Part 2 next. Thanks.